From b059dd00092e9b3d6891f2482cc08dfd3841ce1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 11:14:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Draw a tempo for every turn

The aim eased, spun up and settled, and did all three as a function of the
angle: the same corner walked twice was the same ticks of the same steps,
forever. Every turn now draws a tempo of its own when it starts - 0.85 to
1.15 of the ordinary speed, once per turn rather than once a tick - which
scales how much of what is left the hand takes per tick and how quickly it
spins up. The flick ceiling is left exactly where it was: a tempo decides
how the turn is made, not how fast a hand can move, so nothing it draws can
snap the hand or slow a click's hurry into a drift.

TurnPace holds the draw; AimDynamics.humanStep takes the tempo; LookBehavior
draws one where it resets the age of a turn, in both places it does that.

Doing this uncovered a latent stall in AimDynamics.steppedAngle: the snap to
whole mouse pixels could round a small fraction of what was left down to no
pixel at all while more than a pixel of turn remained, and the aim then sat a
fraction of a degree short of its target as long as it kept being asked for
it. The ordinary half could not reach that state and anything under a half
could. The rule is now that a hand asked to move always takes the pixel, and
AimDynamicsTest pins it for fractions from 0.05 up.

Validation: ./gradlew :test - 288 tests, 0 failures. New TurnPaceTest (5),
six new cases in AimDynamicsTest, one in the fuzz run's arrival sweep which
now runs every turn at the slow end, the ordinary pace and the quick end.
